  "meta": {
    "title": "Squid Fall Redux: Chitin Rush at Depth",
    "date": "2026-10-27",
    "lesson": "When a jumbo squid carcass sinks into the cold dim water of the Gulf of California, its chitinous beak and pen become a sudden feast. Copiotrophic bacteria like Vibrio and Pseudoalteromonas race to colonize the fall, deploying chitinases to crack the polymer into sugars they can absorb. The bloom is explosive but short-lived: as bacterial density spikes, so does phage pressure, and the population can crash within days to a kill-the-winner cycle. This level asks you to exploit a chitin-dominated particle field fast, before viral lysis and patient grazers catch up with your success.",
    "citation": "Simplified from observations of squid-fall microbial succession studies in the Gulf of California (Dosidicus gigas carcasses, 600-900m).",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Gulf of California jumbo squid carcass falls and chitinolytic Vibrio/Pseudoalteromonas bloom-and-crash succession"
  },
